00:49:08,130 --> 00:49:09,489
like if you can get into the gap

1012
00:49:09,489 --> 00:49:11,210
between feeling emotion and feeling,

1013
00:49:11,210 --> 00:49:13,329
you start to figure out, oh, wait a minute,

1014
00:49:13,329 --> 00:49:14,789
that feeling is a frame.

1015
00:49:14,789 --> 00:49:16,969
It's not exactly trustworthy.

1016
00:49:16,969 --> 00:49:17,889
It's different.

1017
00:49:17,889 --> 00:49:19,329
There's two things going on here.

1018
00:49:19,329 --> 00:49:20,610
And the minute that feeling shows up,

1019
00:49:20,610 --> 00:49:22,889
it locks me into a meaning.

1020
00:49:22,889 --> 00:49:25,130
And so when you start doing that work

1021
00:49:25,130 --> 00:49:27,210
and you start noticing there's like,

1022
00:49:27,210 --> 00:49:28,250
there's a difference there,

1023
00:49:28,250 --> 00:49:31,650
I think all these things are very helpful

1024
00:49:31,650 --> 00:49:35,170
sort of on the way to metacognition.

1025
00:49:35,170 --> 00:49:36,969
I wanna tie that back to Josh for a second.

1026
00:49:36,969 --> 00:49:39,889
So it seems like there's a couple of things.

1027
00:49:39,889 --> 00:49:43,469
I'm gonna say how I'm hearing what you're saying

1028
00:49:43,469 --> 00:49:44,769
and maybe you can respond to this.

1029
00:49:44,769 --> 00:49:47,369
So it sounds like a lot of the conditions

1030
00:49:47,369 --> 00:49:49,769
Josh was using were the same conditions

1031
00:49:49,769 --> 00:49:50,929
we need for intuition.

1032
00:49:50,929 --> 00:49:53,809
So, rapid feedback, lot of iterations,

1033
00:49:53,809 --> 00:49:57,530
and a relatively unchanging environment.

1034
00:49:57,530 --> 00:49:59,929
You know, you're confined in what's happening.

1035
00:50:01,449 --> 00:50:05,610
And then, I heard, but you didn't say,

1036
00:50:05,610 --> 00:50:08,130
or maybe I inferred from my past knowledge of Josh,

1037
00:50:08,130 --> 00:50:10,349
that he was okay being uncomfortable,

1038
00:50:10,349 --> 00:50:12,489
which means he was okay looking like a fool

1039
00:50:12,489 --> 00:50:13,650
and being a beginner,

1040
00:50:13,650 --> 00:50:16,329
and he didn't have an image

1041
00:50:16,329 --> 00:50:18,849
or something he had to maintain,

1042
00:50:18,849 --> 00:50:21,230
and he was okay making mistakes

1043
00:50:21,230 --> 00:50:24,349
and then getting the feedback from those mistakes.

1044
00:50:24,349 --> 00:50:25,190
That's interesting.

1045
00:50:25,190 --> 00:50:26,429
I didn't, I wasn't thinking about that.

1046
00:50:26,429 --> 00:50:27,469
That is obviously true.

1047
00:50:27,469 --> 00:50:30,989
I think anybody who's good at learning doesn't like,

1048
00:50:30,989 --> 00:50:33,670
I have a, I don't like being bad in public.

1049
00:50:33,670 --> 00:50:36,429
So I try very hard, like even with, you know,

1050
00:50:36,429 --> 00:50:41,389
when I ski, I try to get as far away from the chairlift.

1051
00:50:41,389 --> 00:50:42,949
Like I will, you know,

1052
00:50:42,949 --> 00:50:45,269
I want to iterate in the woods out of sight.

1053
00:50:45,269 --> 00:50:46,469
I think Josh was a little bit,

1054
00:50:46,469 --> 00:50:49,889
Like, when Josh was transitioning from Tai Chi

1055
00:50:49,889 --> 00:50:52,849
and martial arts, Gracie Jiu Jitsu at that point,

1056
00:50:52,849 --> 00:50:55,769
and moving into surfing and paddle boarding,

1057
00:50:55,769 --> 00:50:58,170
which is, you know, where he is right now,

1058
00:50:58,170 --> 00:51:01,730
he left New York and went down to Costa Rica, right,

1059
00:51:01,730 --> 00:51:04,409
where he doesn't really know anybody he got.

1060
00:51:04,409 --> 00:51:06,849
And some of those access to waves and I got that,

1061
00:51:06,849 --> 00:51:08,929
but I think some of that, it was also like,

1062
00:51:08,929 --> 00:51:10,090
let me get away from everybody I know,

1063
00:51:10,090 --> 00:51:13,389
so I could like be really bad and it doesn't matter.

1064
00:51:13,389 --> 00:51:17,590
So I have a theory on how people learn, just on this notion.

1065
00:51:17,590 --> 00:51:19,909
I think the best people in the world

1066
00:51:19,909 --> 00:51:22,750
often learn in what I call the dark hours.

1067
00:51:22,750 --> 00:51:24,449
Nobody's watching them, there's no boss,

1068
00:51:24,449 --> 00:51:25,869
it doesn't matter what your field is,

1069
00:51:25,869 --> 00:51:28,010
it's how you hone your craft, you play.

1070
00:51:28,010 --> 00:51:31,909
You're there after 5 p.m., it's 10 o'clock at night,

1071
00:51:31,909 --> 00:51:34,130
you're playing around with an Excel spreadsheet,

1072
00:51:34,130 --> 00:51:36,670
or you're riding a wave, or you're doing whatever,

1073
00:51:36,670 --> 00:51:37,969
and nobody's watching you.

1074
00:51:37,969 --> 00:51:39,230
And in those moments you can fail

1075
00:51:39,230 --> 00:51:40,630
because there's no consequences,

1076
00:51:40,630 --> 00:51:42,230
there's no boss directing you,

1077
00:51:42,230 --> 00:51:44,349
and you're playing and you're learning.

1078
00:51:44,349 --> 00:51:46,190
And that sort of ties into what you were saying

1079
00:51:46,190 --> 00:51:48,510
with skiing and how you, yeah,

1080
00:51:48,510 --> 00:51:50,710
I want to do this in the back where nobody can see me.

1081
00:51:50,710 --> 00:51:53,070
I think about this slightly differently.

1082
00:51:53,070 --> 00:51:55,389
Just to say, I always talk about the importance

1083
00:51:55,389 --> 00:51:57,030
of non-time.

1084
00:51:57,030 --> 00:51:58,389
I get up and start my writing day

1085
00:51:58,389 --> 00:52:00,750
about 3.30, four o'clock every day.

1086
00:52:00,750 --> 00:52:04,530
And one of the reasons is nobody bugs me.

1087
00:52:04,530 --> 00:52:08,110
Nobody owns, four a.m. to seven a.m. is non-time.

1088
00:52:08,110 --> 00:52:09,909
It doesn't belong to anybody.

1089
00:52:09,909 --> 00:52:12,750
And I, you know, this is an old habit from,

1090
00:52:12,750 --> 00:52:15,889
I used to live on the West Coast and I was like,

1091
00:52:15,889 --> 00:52:17,349
I was the West Coast reporter

1092
00:52:17,349 --> 00:52:19,030
for a lot of East Coast publications.

1093
00:52:19,030 --> 00:52:20,429
And we were all young and hungry.

1094
00:52:20,429 --> 00:52:23,949
And so like my friends, which my editors would show up

1095
00:52:23,949 --> 00:52:25,110
at like eight o'clock in the morning,

1096
00:52:25,110 --> 00:52:26,730
they'd get to work early.

1097
00:52:26,730 --> 00:52:30,389
And it's five o'clock back on the West Coast

1098
00:52:30,389 --> 00:52:32,030
and they would start to call me.

1099
00:52:32,030 --> 00:52:35,510
So I knew I had to get my work done,

1100
00:52:35,510 --> 00:52:37,949
like my books written before the phone started ringing

1101
00:52:37,949 --> 00:52:38,849
because I was so poor.

1102
00:52:38,849 --> 00:52:41,150
Like the minute they called, what do you need?

1103
00:52:41,150 --> 00:52:41,989
How high?

1104
00:52:41,989 --> 00:52:42,809
I'll jump, right?

1105
00:52:42,809 --> 00:52:43,650
Like, what do you need?

1106
00:52:43,650 --> 00:52:45,090
I'll go do it.

1107
00:52:45,090 --> 00:52:46,550
So it was an instant yes.

1108
00:52:46,550 --> 00:52:47,389
And I knew this.

1109
00:52:47,389 --> 00:52:49,110
So I had to like start getting up earlier

1110
00:52:49,110 --> 00:52:50,989
and earlier and earlier.

1111
00:52:50,989 --> 00:52:54,090
And I started to realize exactly what you did is,

1112
00:52:54,090 --> 00:52:56,550
now I love it because sometimes in my job,

1113
00:52:56,550 --> 00:52:58,110
I'm sure in your job too,

1114
00:52:58,110 --> 00:53:01,150
there are days I got to spend an hour

1115
00:53:01,150 --> 00:53:03,110
or two hours on a sentence.

1116
00:53:03,110 --> 00:53:05,150
And if you're asking me to spend two hours

1117
00:53:05,150 --> 00:53:08,409
on a sentence at 9 a.m. in normal business hours,

1118
00:53:08,409 --> 00:53:11,869
when I got to like run my company, you're crazy.

1119
00:53:11,869 --> 00:53:13,070
I cannot do it.

1120
00:53:13,070 --> 00:53:15,429
And it's certainly not playful, right?

1121
00:53:15,429 --> 00:53:17,789
And really what you want when you're stuck writing

1122
00:53:17,789 --> 00:53:19,829
is exactly that you have to start playing

1123
00:53:19,829 --> 00:53:21,869
with the language to like get that break through.

1124
00:53:21,869 --> 00:53:23,949
You have to come at it from weird angles,

1125
00:53:23,949 --> 00:53:25,190
do all that stuff.

1126
00:53:25,190 --> 00:53:27,389
And you can only do that with like that non-timer,

1127
00:53:27,389 --> 00:53:30,750
your dark, dark, dark hours.

1128
00:53:30,750 --> 00:53:32,989
Yeah, that's sort of how I think about it.

1129
00:53:32,989 --> 00:53:34,429
One of the things we have in common

1130
00:53:34,429 --> 00:53:35,829
is that we both had Lyme.

1131
00:53:35,829 --> 00:53:38,030
Tell me about your story.

1132
00:53:38,030 --> 00:53:42,230
It's sort of the second half of my entrance

1133
00:53:42,230 --> 00:53:44,389
into flow science comes out of this.

1134
00:53:44,389 --> 00:53:48,110
I had this crazy sort of year of sort of,

1135
00:53:48,110 --> 00:53:50,469
it's the last year of where I was a journalist.

1136
00:53:50,469 --> 00:53:51,929
I had a bunch of bad-ass assignments.

1137
00:53:51,929 --> 00:53:54,269
I went to Africa and lived on the side of a river

1138
00:53:54,269 --> 00:53:56,869
and met at Gasgar for a couple of months.

1139
00:53:56,869 --> 00:54:00,429
I was part of the first National Geographic

1140
00:54:00,429 --> 00:54:03,190
sand surfing expedition into the Sahara.

1141
00:54:03,190 --> 00:54:05,110
Then I did the first biotic survey

1142
00:54:05,110 --> 00:54:06,670
ever in a slot canyon.

1143
00:54:06,670 --> 00:54:09,750
It was like all this madness to us.

1144
00:54:09,750 --> 00:54:11,030
And then I went to a wedding,

1145
00:54:11,030 --> 00:54:12,670
a friend's wedding in Long Island

1146
00:54:12,670 --> 00:54:14,190
and went for a walk in the Arboretum

1147
00:54:14,190 --> 00:54:16,190
because I'm shy around people I don't know.

1148
00:54:16,190 --> 00:54:18,710
And he was his friends and I didn't know anybody.

1149
00:54:18,710 --> 00:54:21,750
And I got bit by a tick at a wedding in Long Island.

1150
00:54:21,750 --> 00:54:23,389
But I lost, I spent three years in bed.

1151
00:54:23,389 --> 00:54:24,949
I was one of the first cases in California.

1152
00:54:24,949 --> 00:54:27,710
So they had no idea what was on me.

1153
00:54:27,710 --> 00:54:30,010
And it was, nobody could fix me.

1154
00:54:30,010 --> 00:54:33,469
So that I was on drugs, to my stomach lining.

1155
00:54:33,469 --> 00:54:34,309
I was on doxycycline

1156
00:54:34,309 --> 00:54:36,869
until my stomach started bleeding out.

1157
00:54:36,869 --> 00:54:38,030
And they're like,

1158
00:54:38,030 --> 00:54:40,110
we don't know if you're ever gonna get better.

1159
00:54:41,070 --> 00:54:41,909
We're done with you.

1160
00:54:41,909 --> 00:54:44,469
We can't do anything more for you basically.

1161
00:54:44,469 --> 00:54:45,309
It's so interesting

1162
00:54:45,309 --> 00:54:47,190
because doxycycline is the treatment.

1163
00:54:47,190 --> 00:54:48,150
I know it's the drug,

1164
00:54:48,150 --> 00:54:50,369
but they kept me on it for so long

1165
00:54:50,369 --> 00:54:52,750
that like now they'll keep you,

1166
00:54:52,750 --> 00:54:54,789
it's a month or two months.

1167
00:54:54,789 --> 00:54:57,070
They kept me on it for a year and a half,

1168
00:54:57,070 --> 00:54:58,489
like which was insane.

1169
00:54:58,489 --> 00:55:01,150
So it did a lot of bad stuff to me.

1170
00:55:01,150 --> 00:55:02,869
The story I always tell is I was functional

1171
00:55:02,869 --> 00:55:04,030
like 10% of the time.

1172
00:55:04,030 --> 00:55:05,650
I don't know how sick you got, but I couldn't...

1173
00:55:05,730 --> 00:55:06,730
I was really sick.

1174
00:55:06,809 --> 00:55:09,230
Yeah, my brain stopped working, my short-term memory was gone,

1175
00:55:09,309 --> 00:55:10,650
my long-term memory, all of it.

1176
00:55:10,730 --> 00:55:12,570
And I couldn't, you know, walk across the room,

1177
00:55:12,650 --> 00:55:13,909
and after, I was gonna kill myself.

1178
00:55:13,989 --> 00:55:16,949
I had sleeping pills, I had booze, I had a plan.

1179
00:55:17,030 --> 00:55:19,329
It was just, it was like when, not if.

1180
00:55:19,750 --> 00:55:21,369
And I was in L.A. at the time,

1181
00:55:21,449 --> 00:55:24,210
and a friend of mine showed up, my dressed up,

1182
00:55:24,289 --> 00:55:26,090
and demanded that we go surfing.

1183
00:55:26,829 --> 00:55:28,469
And I was laughing. I was like,

1184
00:55:28,889 --> 00:55:32,429
Andrew, I can't walk across a room, I'm not going surfing.

1185
00:55:32,429 --> 00:55:34,230
And she would not leave my apartment

1186
00:55:34,230 --> 00:55:35,570
and wouldn't leave and wouldn't leave and wouldn't leave

1187
00:55:35,570 --> 00:55:37,269
and after like three hours.

1188
00:55:37,269 --> 00:55:39,530
And you know how irritable you are with Lyme, right?

1189
00:55:39,530 --> 00:55:41,730
You have no reserves.

1190
00:55:41,730 --> 00:55:44,070
And she really just, it didn't matter what I said to her,

1191
00:55:44,070 --> 00:55:45,309
she was not leaving.

1192
00:55:45,309 --> 00:55:46,949
And after three hours, I was like, I give up.

1193
00:55:46,949 --> 00:55:48,170
Let's just go surfing today.

1194
00:55:48,170 --> 00:55:49,710
What is the worst that can happen?

1195
00:55:49,710 --> 00:55:51,489
I'm gonna kill myself anyway, right?

1196
00:55:51,489 --> 00:55:53,789
So she took me to the Pacific.

1197
00:55:53,789 --> 00:55:55,389
They gave me a giant board, right?

1198
00:55:55,389 --> 00:55:57,389
And surfing the bigger board, the easier it is to surf.

1199
00:55:57,389 --> 00:56:00,530
And it was summer, it was a mellow, warm day

1200
00:56:00,530 --> 00:56:02,329
and that people had to, like, take me by the elbows

1201
00:56:02,409 --> 00:56:03,710
and lead me out to the break.

1202
00:56:04,130 --> 00:56:07,170
And I was on my board, like, less than 30 seconds,

1203
00:56:07,250 --> 00:56:08,329
and a wave came.

1204
00:56:08,710 --> 00:56:11,130
And I think I took literally all the energy I had

1205
00:56:11,210 --> 00:56:12,670
in the world and popped into my feet.

1206
00:56:12,750 --> 00:56:14,389
And I had no intention. Like, when we went surfing,

1207
00:56:14,469 --> 00:56:17,809
I was like, I'm gonna sit out in the sun for an hour,

1208
00:56:17,889 --> 00:56:19,469
and then we're gonna go home. I'm not gonna surf.

1209
00:56:19,550 --> 00:56:21,969
That would be insane and impossible.

1210
00:56:22,050 --> 00:56:25,090
And I popped up into a dimension I didn't even know existed.

1211
00:56:25,469 --> 00:56:27,650
I time slowed to an absolute crawl.

1212
00:56:27,650 --> 00:56:31,489
I've been in flow states before, but not a full-blown macro-flow state.

1213
00:56:31,570 --> 00:56:34,869
I started to have an out-of-body experience.

1214
00:56:35,750 --> 00:56:37,789
And a bunch of other really crazy things happened,

1215
00:56:37,869 --> 00:56:40,590
and it felt, it was amazing.

1216
00:56:40,670 --> 00:56:43,329
Because the best part about it is, like, my head was clear,

1217
00:56:43,409 --> 00:56:45,329
I could surf, the pain was gone,

1218
00:56:45,670 --> 00:56:48,670
and it was amazing. I caught four more waves that day,

1219
00:56:48,750 --> 00:56:50,250
and I was done.

1220
00:56:50,750 --> 00:56:52,969
And they drove me home, and they put me into bed,

1221
00:56:53,050 --> 00:56:54,670
and I didn't move for 14 days.

1222
00:56:54,750 --> 00:56:55,710
People had to bring me food,

1223
00:56:55,710 --> 00:56:56,949
because I couldn't even get to my kitchen.

1224
00:56:56,949 --> 00:56:58,750
I was so destroyed from it.

1225
00:56:58,750 --> 00:57:00,909
And on the 15th day, I could walk again.

1226
00:57:00,909 --> 00:57:02,869
I had a neighbor who was a pro surfer,

1227
00:57:02,869 --> 00:57:05,469
a former pro surfer, and I didn't even know him.

1228
00:57:05,469 --> 00:57:07,010
And I just like knocked on his door,

1229
00:57:07,010 --> 00:57:09,090
and I said, hey, can you drive me to the beach?

1230
00:57:09,090 --> 00:57:11,469
And I went surfing again,

1231
00:57:11,469 --> 00:57:13,309
and over the course of like six months,

1232
00:57:13,309 --> 00:57:14,650
I went from about 10% functional

1233
00:57:14,650 --> 00:57:16,150
to about 80% functional.

1234
00:57:16,150 --> 00:57:19,110
And the only thing I was doing was surfing

1235
00:57:19,110 --> 00:57:21,949
and coming back being destroyed.

1236
00:57:21,949 --> 00:57:25,909
And slowly, over time, I start, I got healthier and healthier,

1237
00:57:25,989 --> 00:57:28,289
and I was having these wild altered state experiences

1238
00:57:28,789 --> 00:57:29,750
in the waves.

1239
00:57:29,829 --> 00:57:33,550
And Lyme, as you know, is only fatal if it gets into your brain.

1240
00:57:33,630 --> 00:57:36,170
And I'm a hardcore scientist, I'm a rational materialist,

1241
00:57:36,250 --> 00:57:38,090
I'm having basic quasi-spiritual experiences

1242
00:57:38,170 --> 00:57:39,090
in the waves.

1243
00:57:39,170 --> 00:57:41,429
And I think, okay, I might be feeling better,

1244
00:57:41,510 --> 00:57:42,769
but it's gotta be an illusion,

1245
00:57:42,849 --> 00:57:46,309
the disease is in my brain, and I'm clearly dying.

1246
00:57:46,389 --> 00:57:48,650
So I lit out on a giant quest to figure out

1247
00:57:48,730 --> 00:57:50,110
what the hell happened to me.

1248
00:57:50,110 --> 00:57:54,869
And I had seen flow sort of work earlier in my career with athletes

1249
00:57:54,869 --> 00:57:57,710
and getting them sort of from zero to superhero,

1250
00:57:57,710 --> 00:58:00,250
but I didn't exactly know it was flow.

1251
00:58:00,250 --> 00:58:02,250
We didn't quite know what was going on.

1252
00:58:02,250 --> 00:58:03,710
It was hard to tell what was flow,

1253
00:58:03,710 --> 00:58:05,710
what was a quote-unquote spiritual experience.

1254
00:58:05,710 --> 00:58:07,510
The literature was very confusing.

1255
00:58:07,510 --> 00:58:08,590
The science wasn't there yet.

1256
00:58:08,590 --> 00:58:10,929
The neurobiology wasn't done yet.

1257
00:58:10,929 --> 00:58:13,130
Um, so it was really hard to figure that out,

1258
00:58:13,130 --> 00:58:14,929
but I, you know, quickly figured out

1259
00:58:14,929 --> 00:58:16,429
that these states have a name.

